.TH PACKAGES 1 "2026-08-30" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
packages \- resolve and install the ~/.packages wishlist across package managers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B packages
.RI < command " [" args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B packages
maintains a per-machine package wishlist at
.IR ~/.packages .
The file is seeded by
.BR dots\-setup (1)
from
.I $DOTS_DIR/templates/packages/.packages
on first install and never overwritten again; it records both the baseline
the bootstrap guarantees and every package deliberately added since. The
shim resolves each wishlist entry against whatever package managers are
actually installed and installs what is missing, so a fresh machine
converges from a list of names with no per-platform bookkeeping.
.PP
It is deployed as
.I ~/bin/packages
by
.BR dots\-setup (1)
from
.IR common/bin/bin/packages .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B update
Satisfy every wishlist entry: refresh backend index caches, skip entries
already installed or provided by setup, and resolve + install the rest.
Entries that match no backend exactly are resolved once interactively (see
.BR RESOLUTION ).
.TP
.B status
Report each entry's state \-\- provided, installed, resolved (a cached
choice still to install), not here (pins exclude every available backend),
or no match. Read-only: no installs, no prompts. Always exits 0.
.TP
.BI add " name" \ [ @mgr:pkg " ...]"
Append an entry to
.I ~/.packages
and install it. An entry already named in the wishlist is reported and
not re-added; installing the existing list is left to
.BR update .
.TP
.BI init " [template]"
Seed
.I ~/.packages
from a template file \- by default
.I templates/packages/.packages
in the checkout this shim was deployed from (or
.IR $DOTS_DIR ).
Refuses to overwrite an existing wishlist.
.SH WISHLIST FORMAT
One entry per line; blank lines and comments (a line starting with
.BR # )
are ignored, as is anything after an inline
.BR # .
An entry is one or more whitespace-separated tokens:
.TP
.I name
Try the exact name on every backend, in priority order.
.TP
.IB name " @mgr: a,b"
On backend
.I mgr
try
.IR a ,
then
.IR b ;
unlisted backends still try the bare name.
.TP
.IB name " @mgr:\-"
Never resolve this entry on backend
.IR mgr .
.PP
The first backend offering an exact match (or a pinned alternative) wins.
.SH BACKENDS
Checked in this order; the first available one wins. macOS is brew-only;
on Linux and BSD Homebrew is consulted after the system package manager
(Linuxbrew).
.TP
.B apt
apt-get installs, apt-cache lookups; the index is refreshed
.RI ( "apt-get update" )
before the first resolution.
.TP
.B apk
.B apk add/update .
.TP
.B dnf
Lookups run from the local cache
.RI ( \-\-cacheonly );
distributions refresh their own metadata.
.TP
.B pacman
.B "pacman \-S \-\-needed \-\-noconfirm"
installs; the sync database is refreshed
.RB ( \-Sy )
before the first resolution.
.TP
.B pkg
FreeBSD
.BR pkg .
.TP
.B brew
Homebrew formulas and casks. Brew is intended to run as the user, never
through the privilege prefix.
.PP
Privileged installs run under
.B sudo
or
.B doas
(plainly as root); with neither available, system-package installs fail
with a warning and the run exits 2.
.SH PROVIDED BASELINE
The names
.BR age ", " bash ", " bc ", " coreutils ", " curl ", " direnv ", " fnm ", " git ", " just ", " jq ", " locales ", " less ", " sdkman ", " shellcheck ", " stow ", " tmux ", " uv
and
.B xz-utils
are installed (or equivalent) by
.BR dots\-setup (1).
Wishlist entries with these names are recorded but never resolved or
installed \- silently skipped, so
.BR update 's
output stays about what the user actually wants. Keep them in the file: the
wishlist doubles as the auditable record of what a machine's bootstrap
provides.
.SH RESOLUTION
An entry that no backend matches exactly is offered its search candidates
(bounded per backend and in total) from the non-excluded backends. The
prompt accepts a candidate number to install,
.B s
to record a permanent skip for this machine, or
.B Enter
to leave the entry unresolved for now. Decisions are stored one per line
(name<TAB>backend<TAB>package; backend
.B \-
is a skip record) and applied silently on later runs, so a machine asks at
most once per name. Exact matches are deterministic and never cached.
.PP
The shim never removes anything: a wishlist line deleted only stops being
managed. There is no pruning and no uninstall path.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DOTS_DIR
Repository location used to find the wishlist template (default
.IR ~/.dots ).
.TP
.B PACKAGES_FILE
Overrides the wishlist path (default
.IR ~/.packages ).
.TP
.B PACKAGES_STATE_DIR
Overrides the state directory holding the resolution cache (default
.IR ~/.local/state/packages ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Update/add converged, or status completed.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error or bad wishlist syntax (e.g. two names in one entry).
.TP
.B 2
Completed, but one or more entries are unresolved or failed to install.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.packages
The wishlist (seeded by
.BR dots\-setup (1)).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/packages/resolutions
Cached resolutions and skip records.
.TP
.I ~/bin/packages
The deployed shim
.RI ( common/bin/bin/packages
in the repository).
.TP
.I ~/.dots/templates/packages/.packages
The wishlist template.
.SH EXAMPLES
Converge a fresh machine after editing the wishlist:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo -en "htop\\nhttpie\\nmariadb10\\n" >> ~/.packages
packages update
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pin per-platform spellings so a machine is never asked:
.PP
.RS
.nf
build-essential @apt:build-essential @apk:build-base @dnf:gcc-c++,make @brew:-
postgresql18 @18
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect before installing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
packages status
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dots\-setup (1),
.BR runtimes (1),
.BR stow (1),
.BR brew (1),
.BR apt (8)